WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.956
- New Amsterdam, season
three, episode one, let's go.

00:00:03.956 --> 00:00:06.539
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.441 --> 00:00:09.917
Shout out to everyone
who's gotten their vaccine

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:11.350
#ThisIsOurShot

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.620
is an amazing campaign
of our healthcare workers

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.310
showing themselves getting their shots,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.509
talk about leading by example.

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:19.560
And I hope that everyone talks
to their primary care doctors

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.520
or other health professionals
about getting their vaccines.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.140
Linking my videos on vaccines down below,

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.740
I did one with Mama Doctor
Jones in case you are pregnant

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:27.948
or thinking about
getting pregnant as well.

00:00:27.948 --> 00:00:31.151
- I just wanna tell
everyone how proud I am

00:00:31.151 --> 00:00:32.679
of this whole hospital.

00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:35.430
- I don't know how I feel
about this beard on him.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.137
I think I liked him without the beard.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:39.470
You know, actually him and
I, before the pandemic,

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.630
walked in "Blue Jacket
Fashion Show" to raise money

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:42.463
for prostate cancer.

00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:43.470
He`s quite the model.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.320
- I am committed to fixing
the cracks around here.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.623
Okay the system abandoned
us when we needed it most.

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.456
(clouds roaring)

00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:55.310
And I vow to fix the system
that left us overburdened.

00:00:56.946 --> 00:01:00.010
- Since when do hospitalists
have a conversation

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:00.934
about something very important

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:04.060
like hospital funding in
hallways around patients.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.448
There's conference rooms for a reason.

00:01:05.448 --> 00:01:08.698
(plane engine roaring)

00:01:11.764 --> 00:01:12.980
Okay.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.137
Is this a Sully moment?

00:01:14.137 --> 00:01:19.137
"US Airways 1549 landing on
the freezing cold Hudson River

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.554
all because of heroic Captain Sully

00:01:21.554 --> 00:01:23.119
and his fabulous crew."

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:24.460
(plane engine roaring)
- Whoa!

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.009
this is a Sully moment

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:29.259
(plane engine roaring)

00:01:31.065 --> 00:01:31.945
(water splashing)

00:01:31.945 --> 00:01:32.778
Whoa.

00:01:36.431 --> 00:01:37.889
That look like a fairly smooth landing.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:39.786
I mean, outside of some bumps and bruises

00:01:39.786 --> 00:01:41.594
and depending on how cold
it is, some hypothermia,

00:01:41.594 --> 00:01:44.069
I think everyone should
be pretty safe on board.

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:45.224
(woman wheezes)

00:01:45.224 --> 00:01:46.150
But obviously,

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:46.983
cause it's a medical trauma
(laughing)

00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:48.540
That's not gonna be the case.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.230
- Ma'am we're gonna take
good care of you okay?

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:51.130
Let's go, a Trauma one.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.280
Hey, I need an EKG of portable chest

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.350
and full blood panel,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.183
including co-op.
(woman crys)

00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:57.150
- Ordering blood tests
in a situation like this

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.590
is important but the first
thing you wanna do is get big

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.660
large bore IVs into both
arms to give fluids and blood

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.200
in case the person's bleeding out.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.315
That kind of access is
the most important thing

00:02:07.315 --> 00:02:08.570
to someone's survival.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.604
Obviously airway and breathing
as well but I already saw

00:02:10.604 --> 00:02:13.276
that she is in fact airway
clear and breathing.

00:02:13.276 --> 00:02:15.153
- That's been suspended
with muffled heart cells

00:02:15.153 --> 00:02:16.439
- Tamponade from blunt force trauma.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:19.034
- That means, she's actually
bleeding in an enclosed space

00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:21.135
into the lung cavity,
into the cardiac cavity.

00:02:21.135 --> 00:02:24.257
And as a result, if you have
so much fluid around the heart

00:02:24.257 --> 00:02:25.316
it can't expand fully.

00:02:25.316 --> 00:02:27.860
'Cause when a heart beats
It doesn't just contract.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.100
It actually has to relax.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.450
And if it doesn't have
room to relax and expand

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.578
it actually doesn't get
enough blood flow to itself

00:02:33.578 --> 00:02:34.411
because it's a muscle.

00:02:34.411 --> 00:02:35.869
It needs its own level of circulation.

00:02:35.869 --> 00:02:36.710
(door slams)

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.660
- Co-pilot with deep abdominal
blood lac and blood loss.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.000
Heart rate up to 118, BP down to 108.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.719
- So with the patient who
is bleeding from the abdomen

00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:44.520
has a laceration to the abdomen.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.168
A low blood pressure with
a high pulse can mean

00:02:46.168 --> 00:02:47.744
that that person is
bleeding out, losing blood.

00:02:47.744 --> 00:02:49.057
Because what's essentially happening

00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:52.000
is you're losing pressure
in this closed arterial

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.743
and venous system in
the circulatory system,

00:02:53.743 --> 00:02:56.200
and your heart is trying to compensate

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.450
by increasing the heart
rate to try and get

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.169
more blood flow to the areas that aren't

00:03:00.169 --> 00:03:01.277
getting enough oxygenation.

00:03:01.277 --> 00:03:03.470
It's a compensatory mechanism.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:05.360
- We'll do our best to
stay out of your way.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.880
We just need to interview every
crew member and passenger,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:08.970
who may have pertinent information.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.440
- Well, Roger that.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.273
Oh, well sorry.

00:03:11.273 --> 00:03:13.230
As long as it doesn't
interfere with patient care

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.323
I will help in any way I can.

00:03:14.323 --> 00:03:15.392
- Understood.

00:03:15.392 --> 00:03:18.860
We wanna start with the
pilots, Captain Starks

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.693
- I'll find him for you.

00:03:19.693 --> 00:03:20.526
- Why are they going to the director

00:03:20.526 --> 00:03:21.870
of the hospital for this?

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.580
Like if they just walked in,
went to the head triage person

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.090
in the emergency room, they
can get all this information.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.283
They are law enforcement technically.

00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:31.920
They don't need to go to the
chairperson of the hospital.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.200
- It's your job to investigate
and it's our job to heal.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.033
So once we're done...

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:39.506
- Doctor Goodwin, to clarify

00:03:39.506 --> 00:03:41.576
you're blocking access to a key witness.

00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:44.330
- I'm pretty sure if you
come with a federal subpoena

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.830
or like a judge's order,

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.380
you can get the hospital
to order some blood tests.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.770
But I think a patient
has the right to refuse

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.210
unless it's like one of these

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.811
very specific orders by the court.

00:03:53.811 --> 00:03:56.387
And I think as a pilot, you
sort of give up that right

00:03:56.387 --> 00:03:58.036
and allow them to test your blood.

00:03:58.036 --> 00:03:59.440
I could be wrong here though.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.481
Pilots weigh it.

00:04:00.481 --> 00:04:03.360
(machines beeping)

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.353
- You smell that?

00:04:05.347 --> 00:04:09.113
- Someone chewing gum? candy anything?

00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:10.640
- It's the co-pilot.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.890
It smells like ethylene glycol.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:13.760
- Antifreeze.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:14.593
- When the plane crashed,

00:04:14.593 --> 00:04:16.618
maybe our host snapped
her open abdominal wound.

00:04:16.618 --> 00:04:18.770
- Absorbed it which is why we
can't get the blood to clot.

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:20.190
- To clot, push Fomepizole.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.023
It should act as an antidote

00:04:21.023 --> 00:04:23.680
- That's a very advanced situation

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.261
that a part of the antifreeze
of the plane stabbed him

00:04:26.261 --> 00:04:28.850
in the stomach and is
now pouring that poison

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.310
into his stomach, Jesus, what a story?

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.250
Oh, well he must have been stabbed

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.717
with tubing that had ethylene glycol

00:04:35.717 --> 00:04:38.310
from the airplane and then it pumped

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.864
into his stomach and
then hurt his bile duct

00:04:39.864 --> 00:04:42.770
cause the liver enzymes
were slightly inflamed,

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.083
never happening in reality.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.362
- Liver enzymes are elevated'

00:04:45.362 --> 00:04:46.469
- How high?

00:04:46.469 --> 00:04:47.592
- Three and a half times normal.

00:04:47.592 --> 00:04:49.260
- That's so mild.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.088
Liver enzymes being elevated
three half times normal

00:04:51.088 --> 00:04:52.788
is not great, but it could be just shock

00:04:52.788 --> 00:04:54.246
because it's not getting
enough circulation.

00:04:54.246 --> 00:04:56.160
That's a good time to mention some stuff

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:56.993
about liver enzymes.

00:04:56.993 --> 00:04:58.250
They're called oftentimes LFTs,

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.948
which has liver function tests.

00:04:59.948 --> 00:05:01.873
But the reality is they do not represent

00:05:01.873 --> 00:05:03.683
whether or not your liver
is functioning well.

00:05:03.683 --> 00:05:06.233
They're actually signs of
liver inflammation that

00:05:06.233 --> 00:05:07.307
if you have damage to the liver,

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.092
you actually leak these enzymes.

00:05:09.092 --> 00:05:11.060
And as a result if their elevated,

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.260
your liver function tests

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.442
are saying that there's
damage to your liver.

00:05:14.442 --> 00:05:15.275
(machines beeping)

00:05:15.275 --> 00:05:16.108
- Now what?

00:05:16.108 --> 00:05:16.941
- BP falling, temps dropping.

00:05:16.941 --> 00:05:17.970
- She`s septic.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:18.892
- Start on hand gentle pluzzer

00:05:18.892 --> 00:05:20.283
hang a bag of dopamine
at two mikes a minute.

00:05:20.283 --> 00:05:21.866
- How did they not start that already?

00:05:21.866 --> 00:05:25.370
The patient is open wound from a trauma.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.036
You should have started
them on antibiotics

00:05:27.036 --> 00:05:27.894
broad spectrum.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:29.660
- Enzymes are six times normal.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.860
Liver's not gonna last much longer.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.207
- I need to expose the entire organ.

00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:33.616
We'd need to extend the surgical field

00:05:33.616 --> 00:05:35.387
across the whole abdomen.

00:05:35.387 --> 00:05:36.600
- Not proud of me.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.433
- Scalpel.

00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:38.266
- Do you wanna call in Gastro?

00:05:38.266 --> 00:05:39.500
- No, there's no time.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.242
- You're not getting your hands dirty?

00:05:41.242 --> 00:05:43.989
- Of course not. But,
it's been a few years.

00:05:43.989 --> 00:05:45.660
- You have a steady hand?

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.493
- Yes.

00:05:46.493 --> 00:05:47.326
- Clear field of vision?

00:05:47.326 --> 00:05:48.159
- Yes.

00:05:48.159 --> 00:05:50.440
- Still like that type of place on 17.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.501
- Yes.

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:52.759
- Then what are you waiting for?

00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:54.692
- The last I remember
she was an Oncologist.

00:05:54.692 --> 00:05:55.525
Am I wrong?

00:05:55.525 --> 00:05:57.430
- [male] Yeah, she is an oncologist.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.170
- Why is she operating?

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:00.984
Why is the oncologist operating?

00:06:00.984 --> 00:06:01.817
(door slams)

00:06:01.817 --> 00:06:03.860
- We've got a problem with Shanaz.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.850
(door slams)

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.550
Dissecting aortic aneurysm.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:08.816
Couldn't see it till I
drained out the fluid.

00:06:08.816 --> 00:06:10.030
- That's a problem.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.590
So basically an aneurysm is a
ballooning of the main artery.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.144
That's usually coming off of the aorta,

00:06:15.144 --> 00:06:17.080
which is the main artery
that comes off of the heart.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.680
If you have this ballooning,
meaning that there's a weakness

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.609
in the wall and a
dissecting aortic aneurysm

00:06:21.609 --> 00:06:23.583
means that there's a
tear inside this wall.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:25.380
These ballooning sections of the arteries

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.522
these aneurysms are more likely to tear

00:06:27.522 --> 00:06:29.370
because there's less tissue there.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.203
It's thinner.

00:06:30.203 --> 00:06:31.036
- And what does that mean Dr. Wolch?

00:06:31.036 --> 00:06:33.030
- The aorta could rupture.

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.651
- If it tears, it's basically rupturing.

00:06:34.651 --> 00:06:35.484
(laughs)

00:06:35.484 --> 00:06:37.580
A lot of times the classic
sign is that a patient comes in

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.850
either with very bad chest
pain or very bad back pain.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.340
And sometimes dissecting
aortic aneurysms are missed

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.470
because sometimes doctors
unfortunately misdiagnosis it

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.105
as like back pain or some
kind of sciatic type pain.

00:06:50.105 --> 00:06:52.362
When the reality is they need to rule out

00:06:52.362 --> 00:06:53.852
this dissecting aortic aneurysm,

00:06:53.852 --> 00:06:56.980
which can be done with an x-ray, an EKG

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.564
or a cheetah scan, an ultrasound.

00:06:58.564 --> 00:06:59.397
(machines beeping)

00:06:59.397 --> 00:07:00.230
- What are you doing?

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.670
- We`re not sending another patient off

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.300
into the great unknown
when I can help them

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.980
right here, right now.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.643
- We just paged cardio she's
not your patient anymore.

00:07:06.643 --> 00:07:07.990
- They are all my patients.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:09.920
- How is she gonna perform

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.364
an aortic aneurysm repair
in the, I don't understand

00:07:14.364 --> 00:07:15.671
(machines beeping)

00:07:15.671 --> 00:07:17.820
- The dancing catheter.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.200
Okay, I'm at the aorta.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.610
- Is she really performing
some kind of aortic repair

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:23.443
as an ER physician?

00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:25.671
I don't know, like I've never
heard of this or seen this.

00:07:25.671 --> 00:07:28.350
someone, please tell me if I'm wrong here.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.810
I don't think this is appropriate

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:30.643
- BP is dropping

00:07:30.643 --> 00:07:31.492
(machine beeps)

00:07:31.492 --> 00:07:32.860
dissection's extending.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:33.792
- It`s greater than 200
(door slams)

00:07:33.792 --> 00:07:35.297
- What the hell are you doing?

00:07:35.297 --> 00:07:37.032
- Trying to stop the dissection.

00:07:37.032 --> 00:07:38.460
- Well you didn't, you made it worse.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.653
- I'm trying to fix it.

00:07:39.653 --> 00:07:40.486
- Not fast enough.

00:07:40.486 --> 00:07:42.616
Thanks to you going rogue, I
gotta crack her chest here.

00:07:42.616 --> 00:07:44.430
- The way that they were trying to repair,

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.000
was putting in a catheter
going through the blood vessel

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.000
to go from the interior
portion and attack that problem

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.359
the tear from the inside of the artery,

00:07:52.359 --> 00:07:55.140
without failing, now they have
to go out from the outside

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:56.030
and open the chest.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.590
Which means a longer recovery time

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.597
higher risk, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:58.597 --> 00:08:00.820
- Ma'am, we are authorized to speak

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.940
to any individual who
may have information.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:04.245
- Hey whoa, whoa, whoa enough.

00:08:04.245 --> 00:08:05.157
You don't have the right to come

00:08:05.157 --> 00:08:08.510
into this hospital and rip a
patient away from their doctor.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:09.343
violating HIPAA rules.

00:08:09.343 --> 00:08:10.816
- As a matter of fact, we do.

00:08:10.816 --> 00:08:12.293
(paper clatters)

00:08:12.293 --> 00:08:13.700
- Yeah that's what I was looking for.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:14.533
- What is this?

00:08:14.533 --> 00:08:15.366
- A federal order?

00:08:15.366 --> 00:08:16.199
- I'm kind of proud of myself.

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:17.791
And I think LegalEagle will be proud too

00:08:17.791 --> 00:08:20.430
that I said a federal
judge has to order this.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:21.690
And he said, it's a federal lawyer.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:22.680
- Smart.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:23.513
- Smart.

00:08:23.513 --> 00:08:26.400
- So I'll ask one more
time, where's the pilot?

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.454
- Now you could be in trouble
for obstructing justice.

00:08:28.454 --> 00:08:30.880
- All right, so on take off,

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.359
if a pilot discovers that
his airspeed indicator

00:08:32.359 --> 00:08:34.430
is giving him an inaccurate reading

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:36.050
and he's flying too slow,
what would that pilot do?

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:39.140
- Why is the hospital staff
doing this investigation?

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.620
- Get the nose down.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.519
You go into the dive
in order to gain speed

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:46.494
and then you'd lift up at the very end

00:08:46.494 --> 00:08:49.724
- which is exactly what you did, right?

00:08:49.724 --> 00:08:51.610
- You think that Captain Starks

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:53.757
jeopardized the lives of 150 people,

00:08:53.757 --> 00:08:56.600
We think he saved the lives of 150 people.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.660
- Oh yeah, with this
information that you just got

00:08:58.660 --> 00:08:59.578
five seconds ago.

00:08:59.578 --> 00:09:02.240
